[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4.} 7.Bb3 7...d6 {There are more than 2,000 games with 7...d6. Black wants to undermine e5. We will simply castle and let them take on e5 if they want. We have e-file counterplay. If 8...Nxe5, we want to play 9.Nxe5 and then cxd4. We want to open up lines towards the king in the center.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {8...Be7 is the solid way of developing. We will simply take on d6.} 9.exd6 9...Qxd6 {The queen recaptures so the bishop doesn't move again. And we take on d4 with the knight. We wanted to avoid Bg4.} 10.Nxd4 10...Nxd4 {Material is equal in this line. But we will have an isolated queen's pawn.} 11.cxd4 11...O-O {Black castles.} 12.Nc3 {We will try to limit trading pieces and use our pieces to move around the IQP. With the knight on c3 and bishop on b3, we can even consider pushing d5 safely.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Qe7 {Placing the queen on e7 early, in front of the bishop, seems illogical, but it is the third most popular move here, played 16% of the time. Black pins our pawn on e5 to the king. Capturing in the center is simple and natural. We take our pawn back and protect the center.} 6.cxd4 6...d6 {Black can open up the light-squared bishop and put pressure on e5. It's impractical to defend the pawn with Bf4 now, so I suggest something else. Bb5 essentially serves the same purpose as protecting the pawn on e5, as Black has one less attacker on it now.} 7.Bb5 7...dxe5 {They can still capture once, however. We'll take back...} 8.dxe5 8...Bd7 {And now Black unpins the knight on c6. It seems like our only options to save the pawn are to play Bf4, which is rather passive, or to capture the knight on c6. However, we can castle our king! The point is that Black can't afford to capture the pawn now because of Re1 problems, and now the threat of capturing on f6 is reintroduced.} 9.O-O 9...Ng4 {That's why jumping to g4 is the most logical for Black. We don't have to worry about the e5-pawn for now, though.} 10.Bg5 {We can simply develop our dark-squared bishop with tempo on the queen, and Black is in trouble. Blocking with ...f6 leads to a disaster on the e-file after we capture, so Black has to play something like ...Qc5. After that, we'll simply play Nc3 and enjoy an easy position and a winning advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4.} 7.Bb3 7...dxc3 {Our retreat allows them to capture our pawn on c3. That's fine. We'll recapture with the knight.} 8.Nxc3 8...Bb4 {One of the logical moves for Black is to pin our knight on c3. Let's just bring the king to safety.} 9.O-O 9...Bxc3 {Black capturing on c3 can actually lead them into trouble. We'll take back first.} 10.bxc3 10...O-O {If they try to play logically and castle, thinking they have no worries... Suddenly Bg5 arrives! This is why it's important for Black to move the d-pawn before this.} 11.Bg5 11...Qe8 {The queen's only available square is e8.} 12.Re1 {After which we can play Re1. We've got a big lead in activity and development and a winning advantage. This initiative could transform into either an attack on the king, or a slow positional build up until we squeeze Black.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4.} 7.Bb3 7...dxc3 {Our retreat allows them to capture our pawn on c3. That's fine. We'll recapture with the knight.} 8.Nxc3 8...Bb4 {One of the logical moves for Black is to pin our knight on c3. Let's just bring the king to safety.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black chooses to do the same. Since they haven't captured the c3-knight now... We can choose to protect it with the queen and potentially avoid our pawns splitting. However, there is an additional point behind this move...} 10.Qd3 10...d6 {Expect Black to move the d-pawn at some point and challenge your e5-pawn. However, ...d6 in this case is a mistake! Remember that you can push the pawn to e6 in this position. Why is this possible?} 11.e6 11...Bxe6 {Once Black captures on e6... There's the extremely strong Ng5. Not only do we attack the bishop twice, but we also threaten checkmate on h7!} 12.Ng5 12...g6 {Black has to defend against checkmate, of course. Now we'll cash in on the e6-square.} 13.Bxe6 13...fxe6 {Black recaptures. And we'll take with the knight and a fork!} 14.Nxe6 14...Qh4 {Black tries to play actively with ...Qh4.} 15.Nxf8 {But we've just won an exchange and have a huge advantage. Try to remember this e6 trick when you've got the pieces set up in the right way.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4.} 7.Bb3 7...dxc3 {Our retreat allows them to capture our pawn on c3. That's fine. We'll recapture with the knight.} 8.Nxc3 8...Bb4 {One of the logical moves for Black is to pin our knight on c3. Let's just bring the king to safety.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black chooses to do the same. Since they haven't captured the c3-knight now... We can choose to protect it with the queen and potentially avoid our pawns splitting. However, there is an additional point behind this move...} 10.Qd3 10...Bxc3 {If Black captures on c3, we won't recapture with the queen immediately. We'll play Ng5 first and threaten checkmate!} 11.Ng5 11...g6 {Black can defend against it, but the point of this line is to weaken Black's pawn structure. We can additionally do that here with Qh3.} 12.Qh3 12...h5 {Black is forced to play ...h5 if they don't want to give up the queen.} 13.Qxc3 {And only now can we capture on c3. After this combination, Black's kingside structure is extremely weak and the g6-pawn isn't even defended by the f7-pawn, as it's pinned. We'll look to activate the rest of the pieces and target those weak spots. White's got a big advantage here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4.} 7.Bb3 7...d5 {With this move there are nearly 3,000 games. Black wants central space. We will take on d4 and get a big center.} 8.cxd4 8...Bg4 {8...Bg4 pins our knight. And tries to undermine our defense of d4. We will support d4 further.} 9.Be3 9...Bb4+ {Black speeds up development with this check. But we can develop and block.} 10.Nc3 10...O-O {Black castles. We would love to get that bishop pair.} 11.h3 11...Bh5 {But Black will usually go backwards to h5 and maintain the pin.} 12.O-O {And now we castle: we will go g4 and Qe2 to connect our rooks. We should be happy with our strong central pawns.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4.} 7.Bb3 7...d6 {There are more than 2,000 games with 7...d6. Black wants to undermine e5. We will simply castle and let them take on e5 if they want. We have e-file counterplay. If 8...Nxe5, we want to play 9.Nxe5 and then cxd4. We want to open up lines towards the king in the center.} 8.O-O 8...dxe5 {If the pawn takes on e5, we don't mind. Our goal is to open up lines and attack f7 and potentially the e-file. We can begin to attack f7.} 9.Ng5 9...Nd5 {Black can block the attack on f7 with their knight. However, we will simply open up lines towards their king.} 10.cxd4 10...Nxd4 {10...Nxd4 is a solid approach. The centralized knight keeps the e-file closed for the time being, and it prevents our Qf3 move. We will make use of the e-file to counterattack and claim some compensation.} 11.Re1 11...Nxb3 {This trade gives up a pawn. However, Black's point is that they're getting rid of our powerful bishop. We take the pawn with check.} 12.Rxe5+ 12...Be6 {The bishop blocks, lending extra defense to the knight on d5 in the process.} 13.Qxb3 {The engine suggest we give a check on b5 if we can before taking on b7. That's because we want to provoke Qd7 so that Qxb7 comes with a tempo. We have good open lines and we will also trade our knight for that bishop which is currently holding everything together.} *

