...dxe5 {If the pawn takes on e5, we don't mind. Our goal is to open up lines and attack f7 and potentially the e-file. We can begin to attack f7.} 9.Ng5 9...Nd5 {Black can block the attack on f7 with their knight. However, we will simply open up lines towards their king.} 10.cxd4 10...exd4 {If the pawn recaptures, we've got a nice double attack.} 11.Qf3 {We are completely winning here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Nd5 {Played 32% of the time and in almost 600,000 games, 5...Nd5 is the most popular response for Black. This move is played with a concrete goal of keeping the knight safe but active. Capturing on d4 now could be played, but we give Black some chances after ...Bb4+. Instead, we attack the knight with Bc4...} 6.Bc4 6...Nb6 {It can rotate to the queenside and hit our bishop. The b3-square is where we want to retreat to most of the time. We did give Black a tempo in this small skirmish, through our bishop retreat. 7...Be7 allows us the calm 8.O-O, and we have ideas of playing 9.cxd4.} 7.Bb3 7...d6 {There are more than 2,000 games with 7...d6. Black wants to undermine e5. We will simply castle and let them take on e5 if they want. We have e-file counterplay. If 8...Nxe5, we want to play 9.Nxe5 and then cxd4. We want to open up lines towards the king in the center.} 8.O-O 8...dxe5 {If the pawn takes on e5, we don't mind. Our goal is to open up lines and attack f7 and potentially the e-file. We can begin to attack f7.} 9.Ng5 9...Be6 {The bishop can block. We will take the bishop and get a pawn back.} 10.Nxe6 10...fxe6 {Black recaptures. We take on e6.} 11.Bxe6 11...dxc3 {Black can get greedy. But open lines are what we want. Pawns aren't important in this variation: rather, getting our pieces active is what we must do.} 12.Qg4 12...cxb2 {If Black continues taking pawns, we have so much activity that the engine says we're equal.} 13.Bxb2 {We recapture with the bishop. We will play Nc3 and Rad1 and Rfe1. Ne4 could also be a great follow-up to Nc3.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 11"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Ne4 {...Ne4 is played 14% of the time on move 5. Black wants an active knight in the center and wants to play ...d5 to support it. Of course, we can easily attack that knight while developing a piece, which is more often than not a good choice in the opening.} 6.Qc2 6...d5 {Black goes ahead with their plan and protects the knight with ...d5. Here, Black would like to play ...Bf5, which is why I don't recommend cxd4. Let's instead develop the bishop and pin Black's knight on c6. This way we threaten to capture on d4 with the knight in case Black plays ...Bf5.} 7.Bb5 7...dxc3 {That's why Black removes our pawn on c3. We'll recapture with the knight in order to activate another piece. Now Black's knight on e4 is under additional pressure. Once again, if Black tries ...Bf5, Nd4 is strong.} 8.Nxc3 8...Nxc3 {You will see a trade on c3 here most likely. We'll take back with the queen to keep the pawns together and attack the knight on c6.} 9.Qxc3 9...Bd7 {The most natural defense is with the bishop on d7. This gives us the freedom to castle.} 10.O-O 10...Be7 {Black continues developing naturally, preparing to castle themselves. However, this bishop left its post on f8 and isn't guarding the g7-pawn anymore... Which means that we can push e6! Keep this trick in mind when there's a queen on c3. We give up the e-pawn in exchange for the g7-pawn.} 11.e6 11...fxe6 {Black has no choice but to capture on e6 as the bishop was under attack as well. And now we'll take on g7 and hit the rook in the corner.} 12.Qxg7 12...Bf6 {There's an active defense for Black in the form of ...Bf6.} 13.Qg3 {However, placing the queen on g3 is convenient for us, as it's safe there. Black most likely won't be castling kingside. For the middlegame, active and natural moves are Bf4 to line up with the queen on g3 and hit the c7-pawn, and bringing the rooks to the center. All in all, an interesting but equal position.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 1: Black Takes On d4 (4...exd4) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/5e0ccd53-bbaa-4387-8ccf-ceeaa3308fbc/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular first move for Black! The knight comes out to its natural f3-square and attacks the center.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and defends e5. We play c3, the Ponziani, with the idea of playing d4 next. This in turn can offer open lines and extremely active piece play down the line. A downside could be that the c3-square is now occupied and we can't place a knight there...} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black plays a rather natural move, attacking the center, which takes advantage of the fact that we can't defend the e4-pawn with our knight. The Jaenisch Counterattack is played 45% of the time, in almost 8 million games. We'll gambit a pawn on e4, but we obtain a strong center and put pressure on Black's e5-pawn.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {This study will deal with the most popular response for Black in this position, which is capturing on d4, played 66% of the time. We'll unleash an intermezzo and push in the center with tempo! This move takes advantage of the extra space immediately, prevents Black from capturing on c3 or on e4, and forces Black's knight to move!} 5.e5 5...Ng4 {5...Ng4 looks aggressive and is the second most popular move played 22% of the time. It seems like Black is targeting the e5-pawn, but this gives us the freedom we strive for in the Ponziani.} 6.cxd4 {We'll simply capture on d4, protect the pawn and maintain a massive center. We'll play h3 next to kick the g4-knight away and already achieve a substantial advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Bc5 {Played 16% of the time is the ultra sharp piece sacrifice with 5...Bc5! This is where you need to know your stuff. One wrong move from either side can result in total devastation. We will accept Black's offering. Luckily, Black is the one who is going to have to prove that they have got enough compensation for the piece.} 6.dxc6 6...Bxf2+ {Most often, Black takes on f2 with their bishop and checks our king. Our only legal move is Ke2. A bongcloud, but up a piece.} 7.Ke2 7...Bb6 {Usually, Black retreats the bishop. We'll move our queen to d5. This move attacks Black's knight, but also potentially enables us to capture the b7-pawn at the right moment.} 8.Qd5 8...Nf2 {Black proceeds with ...Nf2, saving their knight and attacking our rook. We'll go Rg1, saving the rook.} 9.Rg1 9...O-O {Black's best is to castle kingside. This is another piece sacrifice... We can capture the b7-pawn and fork!} 10.cxb7 10...Bxb7 {Black has to recapture the b7-pawn. And we'll capture the bishop. We are up a bishop and a knight, but our analysis can't stop here. Black has a strong initiative and we need to know the best way to deal with it.} 11.Qxb7 11...Qf6 {Black activates their queen. ...e4 is coming next and our king could run into some trouble... The best way to proceed is Na3. Our defensive idea is to get the knight to c4 and cover up our king with Be3.} 12.Na3 12...e4 {Black advances their e-pawn and attacks our knight. Trying to hang onto the f3-knight is too dangerous. Instead, we will bring the a3-knight to help defend the king.} 13.Nc4 13...exf3+ {Black captures the knight with check. We ought to recapture with the queen.} 14.Qxf3 14...Rae8+ {And when Black gives a check...} 15.Be3 {We are just in time to cover the king up with our bishop. At the end of this forced mess, the engine gives total equality, but it's Black who needs to play accurately in order to prove that this really is equal. It's easy for Black to go wrong and White has great practical chances. After all, we are up a whole piece!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Bc5 {Played 16% of the time is the ultra sharp piece sacrifice with 5...Bc5! This is where you need to know your stuff. One wrong move from either side can result in total devastation. We will accept Black's offering. Luckily, Black is the one who is going to have to prove that they have got enough compensation for the piece.} 6.dxc6 6...Nxf2 {Played roughly 36% of the time is Black's only other option, ...Nxf2. This really isn't all that great, but you need to know how to punish it. We'll move our queen to d5. Similarly to the ...Bxf2+ lines, this move attacks a piece of Black's (the bishop in this case) and allows for a potential cxb7 at the right moment.} 7.Qd5 7...Qe7 {Black can also defend the bishop. Against this, it's best to capture the b7-pawn and fork Black's pieces!} 8.cxb7 8...Bxb7 {Black has to capture the b7-pawn. We'll capture the bishop. Similar to the ...Bxf2+ line, we need to keep analyzing to know how to best deal with Black's strong initiative.} 9.Qxb7 9...O-O {Black's best is to castle kingside. We'll go Rg1, saving the rook.} 10.Rg1 10...e4 {Black advances their e-pawn and attacks our knight. The idea is to dislodge the knight from f3 and free up the h4-square. We'll go Nd4, saving the knight.} 11.Nd4 11...Qh4 {Black moves their queen into h4, threatening some discoveries. We'll respond with g3, attacking the queen and blocking the diagonal.} 12.g3 12...Qxh2 {Black captures the h2-pawn with their queen and attacks our rook. We'll go Rg2, saving our rook and attacking Black's knight and queen.} 13.Rg2 13...Nd3+ {Black has to give a check with their knight in order not to lose material. We can now slide our king over to d1.} 14.Kd1 14...Qh1 {And when Black moves their queen to h1 and attacks our bishop...} 15.Nd2 {We are just in time with Nd2, defending the bishop on f1. White is up material (two pieces for three pawns) and is much better. We're a little tangled up, but our plan to untangle is Re2, followed by Kc2. The natural-looking ...e3 would now allow us to simply retreat our queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Ne7 {By far the most common response is 5...Ne7, played 55% of the time. Black's knight was attacked, so they move it back to e7 with the intent to bring it over to g6 at some point. Seems like a safe choice, but Black is still far from safety in this position... We'll continue by simply capturing the en prise e5-pawn with our knight.} 6.Nxe5 6...d6 {Shockingly, the most natural way to proceed is a horrible blunder! 6...d6 is played 63% of the time and lands Black into a dead lost position. It's sort of logical as Black attacks the knight and opens up their bishop. But we can punish Black for opening up the diagonal to their king by giving a check with our bishop!} 7.Bb5+ 7...c6 {The only reasonable way to proceed is to block the check with ...c6. We'll continue by taking the c6-pawn. We are now threatening to win lots of material with cxb7+, so Black has to try to do something about it...} 8.dxc6 8...bxc6 {If Black simply recaptures... We can capture the c6-pawn with our knight, threatening more discoveries.} 9.Nxc6 9...Qb6 {Black's best try is to go for ...Qb6. This attacks the b5-bishop and threatens checkmate in one. Looks scary, but we have a move to deal with both of these threats.} 10.Nd4+ {We can simply bring our knight back to d4, defending the bishop and opening up a discovered check. A completely winning position. We are up a pawn and ready to castle. Whatever Black does, they'll be left with a weak king in the center of the board that's subject to attacks.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Ne7 {By far the most common response is 5...Ne7, played 55% of the time. Black's knight was attacked, so they move it back to e7 with the intent to bring it over to g6 at some point. Seems like a safe choice, but Black is still far from safety in this position... We'll continue by simply capturing the en prise e5-pawn with our knight.} 6.Nxe5 6...d6 {Shockingly, the most natural way to proceed is a horrible blunder! 6...d6 is played 63% of the time and lands Black into a dead lost position. It's sort of logical as Black attacks the knight and opens up their bishop. But we can punish Black for opening up the diagonal to their king by giving a check with our bishop!} 7.Bb5+ 7...c6 {The only reasonable way to proceed is to block the check with ...c6. We'll continue by taking the c6-pawn. We are now threatening to win lots of material with cxb7+, so Black has to try to do something about it...} 8.dxc6 8...Qb6 {A tricky attempt is ...Qb6, attacking the b5-bishop and threatening mate. This move, however, fails miserably and for quite a beautiful reason. We can capture the b7-pawn and uncover a check from our bishop. If Black now takes the bishop we can simply take on a8 and make a queen.} 9.cxb7+ 9...Kd8 {Black can attempt one final trick with ...Kd8. Make sure not to prematurely capture the rook and blunder mate! There are many ways to win here, but one is more beautiful than the others. We capture on f7 with our knight.} 10.Nxf7+ 10...Kc7 {Black's only legal move is ...Kc7.} 11.bxa8=N+ {And we finally capture the rook on a8, underpromoting our pawn to a knight and forking Black's king and queen! Black should resign here.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Ne7 {By far the most common response is 5...Ne7, played 55% of the time. Black's knight was attacked, so they move it back to e7 with the intent to bring it over to g6 at some point. Seems like a safe choice, but Black is still far from safety in this position... We'll continue by simply capturing the en prise e5-pawn with our knight.} 6.Nxe5 6...Ng6 {...Ng6 is clearly the better option and is played roughly 16% of the time. Black wants to trade the knights and continue with natural development. Against this, I suggest we play Qe2 and attack the knight on e4.} 7.Qe2 7...Qe7 {The other option is ...Qe7. This is slightly less popular, but still played roughly 33% of the time. We'll proceed by taking the g6-knight.} 8.Nxg6 8...hxg6 {Black recaptures with the h-pawn. We should then continue by developing our bishop to e3.} 9.Be3 9...d6 {Black's most logical way to continue is ...d6, opening up the c8-bishop.} 10.Na3 {And we'll prepare queenside castling with Na3. The knight can jump into c4 or b5 at some point in the future. White is slightly better. We have more space and a lead in development.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Ne7 {By far the most common response is 5...Ne7, played 55% of the time. Black's knight was attacked, so they move it back to e7 with the intent to bring it over to g6 at some point. Seems like a safe choice, but Black is still far from safety in this position... We'll continue by simply capturing the en prise e5-pawn with our knight.} 6.Nxe5 6...Ng6 {...Ng6 is clearly the better option and is played roughly 16% of the time. Black wants to trade the knights and continue with natural development. Against this, I suggest we play Qe2 and attack the knight on e4.} 7.Qe2 7...Nxe5 {Most often, Black takes the e5-knight. We'll capture the e4-knight.} 8.Qxe4 8...Qe7 {Black now has to proceed with ...Qe7, defending and unpinning the knight. We can continue naturally developing with Be2, preparing kingside castling.} 9.Be2 9...d6 {Black's most logical way to continue is ...d6, supporting the knight and opening up the light-squared bishop.} 10.O-O {And we can castle our king. White is slightly better here. We have more space and superior development. We can look to proceed with moves like Na3 and Re1, and should avoid any queen trades if possible.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 7"]
