1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Bc5 {Played 16% of the time is the ultra sharp piece sacrifice with 5...Bc5! This is where you need to know your stuff. One wrong move from either side can result in total devastation. We will accept Black's offering. Luckily, Black is the one who is going to have to prove that they have got enough compensation for the piece.} 6.dxc6 6...Nxf2 {Played roughly 36% of the time is Black's only other option, ...Nxf2. This really isn't all that great, but you need to know how to punish it. We'll move our queen to d5. Similarly to the ...Bxf2+ lines, this move attacks a piece of Black's (the bishop in this case) and allows for a potential cxb7 at the right moment.} 7.Qd5 7...Bb6 {Black can retreat the bishop to safety. We'll respond by saving our rook.} 8.Rg1 8...bxc6 {Black's best is to capture the c6-pawn, preventing any cxb7 ideas. We'll respond by taking the e5-pawn.} 9.Qxe5+ 9...Kf8 {Black really shouldn't trade queens, so the best course of action is ...Kf8.} 10.Nd4 {And we can jump our knight into d4. We're up a piece for two pawns and Black doesn't have a clear attack or way to regain the material.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Bc5 {Played 16% of the time is the ultra sharp piece sacrifice with 5...Bc5! This is where you need to know your stuff. One wrong move from either side can result in total devastation. We will accept Black's offering. Luckily, Black is the one who is going to have to prove that they have got enough compensation for the piece.} 6.dxc6 6...Bxf2+ {Most often, Black takes on f2 with their bishop and checks our king. Our only legal move is Ke2. A bongcloud, but up a piece.} 7.Ke2 7...bxc6 {Black can also capture the c6-pawn. This is played roughly 28% of the time and isn't all that great for Black. We'll proceed with Qa4, activating our queen and attacking the e4-knight, also stopping ...Ba6+.} 8.Qa4 8...f5 {The knight can't move because it's defending the f2-bishop, so Black must defend it by advancing ...f5. We can now go Nbd2, simply looking to trade off Black's key knight on e4.} 9.Nbd2 9...Nxd2 {It is logical for Black to trade knights.} 10.Bxd2 {And we'll recapture with our bishop. White is crushing here. The bishop and the e5-pawn are hanging, and Bg5 is a serious threat to win Black's queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Nxe4 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/3341b221-5530-43ed-a585-9139443a988e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5, the most popular response to 1. e4. We develop our kingside knight to the most natural square, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops their knight and defends the central pawn. And we go for the Ponziani with 3. c3. The idea is, once again, to prepare a quick central expansion with d4.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {Black responds by developing their other knight to f6 and attacking e4. This move signifies the Jaenisch Counterattack, which is the most popular response to the Ponziani. Black's move intends to make use of the fact that we no longer have a convenient way to support e4. But instead of supporting e4, we'll follow up on our previous move and strike in the center with d4!} 4.d4 4...Nxe4 {This study will deal with the second most popular response on move 4, which happens 14% of the time. This capture often leads to some incredibly sharp and dynamic positions where a high degree of accuracy is required. Instead of taking on e5, we'll advance with d5, taking some more space and attacking Black's knight!} 5.d5 5...Nb8 {This retreat is played 18% of the time, but it's Black admitting that they are worse. We can take the pawn.} 6.Nxe5 6...Qe7 {Black could try to set something up on the e-file. But we can defend our knight.} 7.Qd4 7...Nf6 {Black pins our knight.} 8.Be3 {But we can unpin. We will develop with Bc4 and Nd2, with a small advantage thanks to the extra space and development.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 4...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/b570a099-cb32-486f-867c-391423fb47d1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black plays e5. We attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {They defend. And we go for the Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {The knight goes to f6. And we play 4.d4, with a powerful center. We've seen variations with 4...Nxe4 and ...exd4.} 4.d4 4...d6 {But 4...d6 defends e5. It has been played in more than half a million games, which amounts to 12% of the time. Our e-pawn is hanging, but we can afford to play 5.h3 and prevent ...Bg4. You will see why.} 5.h3 5...Be7 {Finally, there's always normal development, which happens 23% of the time. Black wants to follow opening principles and castle quickly. We will gain space and attack the knight.} 6.d5 6...Nb8 {The bishop went to e7, so the knight doesn't have that square. Finally, we defend our e4-pawn.} 7.Bd3 7...O-O {Black castles. They have a position that looks solid, but with less space.} 8.c4 {We will play O-O, Nc3 and Be3 to develop. We will use our greater space advantage to play on both sides of the board.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 4...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/b570a099-cb32-486f-867c-391423fb47d1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black plays e5. We attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {They defend. And we go for the Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {The knight goes to f6. And we play 4.d4, with a powerful center. We've seen variations with 4...Nxe4 and ...exd4.} 4.d4 4...d6 {But 4...d6 defends e5. It has been played in more than half a million games, which amounts to 12% of the time. Our e-pawn is hanging, but we can afford to play 5.h3 and prevent ...Bg4. You will see why.} 5.h3 5...exd4 {Most games go like this. In particular, 36%. We simply recapture.} 6.cxd4 6...Nxe4 {If the knight takes what seems to be a free pawn...} 7.d5 {We attack the knight on c6 and get ready to play the fork with Qa4+. The only move to save the game is the complex 7...Qe7. After which we will block the e-file with 8.Be2 and then play Qa4+, followed by 0-0 and getting a rook to the recently opened e-file. Despite the pawn, we have an advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: Jaenisch Counterattack (3...Nf6) / Study 3: Black Plays 4...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/363f6e24-2176-4ad3-907a-f5afdac7cb85/studies/b570a099-cb32-486f-867c-391423fb47d1/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black plays e5. We attack the pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {They defend. And we go for the Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...Nf6 {The knight goes to f6. And we play 4.d4, with a powerful center. We've seen variations with 4...Nxe4 and ...exd4.} 4.d4 4...d6 {But 4...d6 defends e5. It has been played in more than half a million games, which amounts to 12% of the time. Our e-pawn is hanging, but we can afford to play 5.h3 and prevent ...Bg4. You will see why.} 5.h3 5...Nxe4 {5...Nxe4 right away is a blunder. And it's played almost a quarter of the time!} 6.d5 {In this variation, there is no ...Qe7. We will win a knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} 5.cxd4 5...Bb4+ {Black gives a check. We'll block with the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...d5 {There are more than 120,000 games in this variation. That means that there's a high chance you might see this. We will recapture because there's no danger to having Black's queen on d5.} 7.exd5 7...Qxd5 {The queen recaptures. Since the knight is pinned, this is safe for them.} 8.Be2 {We will castle. Be2 is also a great square because we make sure our knight doesn't get pinned. We'll put a rook on the e-file, and play a3 to see if we get the bishop pair.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} 5.cxd4 5...Bb4+ {Black gives a check. We'll block with the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and pressures the e4-pawn. There are nearly 300,000 games here. Let's create a bigger threat against Black! We gain space in the center with a tempo.} 7.d5 7...Nxe4 {Black ignores the threat on the c6-knight and captures on e4, putting pressure on c3. Qc2 defends everything!} 8.Qc2 8...Nxc3 {Black trades. We recapture, and now two pieces are under attack.} 9.bxc3 9...Qf6 {Black pins and attacks the c3-pawn.} 10.Bb2 {But Bb2 saves the day. We'll win a piece here as Black can't defend against all the threats.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} 5.cxd4 5...Bb4+ {Black gives a check. We'll block with the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and pressures the e4-pawn. There are nearly 300,000 games here. Let's create a bigger threat against Black! We gain space in the center with a tempo.} 7.d5 7...Ne7 {Black retreats. With Bd3 we develop a piece, protect the pawn on e4 and prepare to castle.} 8.Bd3 8...O-O {Black castles. And we'll push even further! Our pawns are very flexible, gaining space with tempo.} 9.e5 9...Nfxd5 {Looks like we've lost a pawn thanks to the pin on c3... ...but a bishop sacrifice saves the day! In fact, this is the Greek Gift sacrifice.} 10.Bxh7+ 10...Kxh7 {Black captures. A strong attack is coming after Ng5+.} 11.Ng5+ 11...Kg8 {Black retreats to "safety". But we are threatening checkmate.} 12.Qh5 12...Re8 {Black makes an escape square for their king. Check.} 13.Qh7+ 13...Kf8 {Only move. We check again.} 14.Qh8+ 14...Ng8 {Black must block. But a check on h7 forces the king out to e7. This is a forced line after ...Kg8.} 15.Nh7+ 15...Ke7 {The only move.} 16.O-O {And we simply castle here. Black is up a piece, but we are doing amazingly well here. Black's king isn't safe and we have many threats. For example, we are threatening the knight on d5, as well as capturing on g7 and Bg5! White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} 5.cxd4 5...Bb4+ {Black gives a check. We'll block with the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. There are more than 230,000 games with this move. Let's attack the knight now and expand in the center.} 7.d5 7...Nce7 {Black retreats the knight and keeps the knight on c3 pinned...} 8.Qa4+ {But we win a piece! Good game.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} 5.cxd4 5...Bb4+ {Black gives a check. We'll block with the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. There are more than 230,000 games with this move. Let's attack the knight now and expand in the center.} 7.d5 7...Bxc3+ {Black is forced to trade on c3. We recapture.} 8.bxc3 8...Nce7 {Black retreats.} 9.h3 {Here, h3 is a nice move to prevent the bishop from pinning our knight. We have a nice position here with a solid and a spacious center. Bd3 with O-O coming.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} 5.cxd4 5...Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to b6 instead. You know the drill. The c3-square is free so let's develop the queenside knight!} 6.Nc3 6...d6 {Black opens up the light-squared bishop. We prevent ...Bg4, which would be annoying.} 7.h3 7...Nf6 {Black develops. We'll develop as well, pinning the knight. The threat is d5.} 8.Bb5 8...Bd7 {Black breaks the pin. Let's get the king to safety.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles as well. And we pin the other knight as well.} 10.Bg5 10...h6 {Black is done allowing these pins, so they attack the bishop. Keeping the pin is important here, though, as it is very annoying for Black.} 11.Bh4 11...g5 {So they decide to attack the bishop further, but weakening their kingside. Do we have a positional sacrifice here? You guessed it! Let's sac the knight.} 12.Nxg5 12...hxg5 {Black recaptures.} 13.Bxg5 {And the pin prevails! The king is weak and Nd5 is unstoppable. The Ponziani allows for quick and sharp development, often catching Black off guard. White is winning already.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...Bb6 {Black retreats the bishop to b6 right away, which rarely happens (only 4% of the time), but should be covered anyway. It is logical as Black keeps the bishop on the g1-a7 diagonal, eyeballing the f2-pawn. We'll push to remove the defender of the e5-pawn!} 5.d5 5...Nb8 {The knight retreats to b8, hoping to reroute via d7 in the future. Having kicked that knight back, let's gain space on the queenside and threaten to annoy the dark-squared bishop. We'll do this before capturing on e5 because a5 is a serious threat.} 6.a4 6...a6 {Black frees up the a7-square. Let's grab the e-pawn.} 7.Nxe5 7...Qe7 {Black attacks the knight, which was their idea all along. We won't be able to save the e4-pawn. But c4 is a nice square for the knight, attacking that bishop.} 8.Nc4 8...Qxe4+ {Black captures. We can safely enter a queenless middlegame, since we are so far ahead in development and have more space.} 9.Qe2 9...Qxe2+ {Black trades. We recapture.} 10.Bxe2 10...Bc5 {Black doesn't want to lose the bishop pair. Developing with an attack on the c7-pawn. Developing with tempo is always good.} 11.Bf4 11...d6 {Black protects. And now we expand on the queenside with a tempo. Now Black has to move their bishop multiple times. And, we are preparing something devious.} 12.b4 12...Ba7 {Retreating. Out of nowhere, White has a strong piece sacrifice!} 13.Bxd6 13...cxd6 {Black grabs the piece.} 14.Nxd6+ {Check! Black cannot protect both f7-pawn and the bishop on c7. White is winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...Bc5 {Black abandons the idea of developing the knight with 3...Nf6, but rather controls the d4-square by bringing the bishop out to c5. This is played more than 25% of the time. Let's push nonetheless! We open up the dark-squared bishop and gain tempo on Black's c5-bishop.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades 93% of the time. We recapture and get the two nice central pawns.} 5.cxd4 5...Bb4+ {Black gives a check. We'll block with the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black develops the knight and pressures the e4-pawn. There are nearly 300,000 games here. Let's create a bigger threat against Black! We gain space in the center with a tempo.} 7.d5 7...Ne7 {Black retreats. With Bd3 we develop a piece, protect the pawn on e4 and prepare to castle.} 8.Bd3 8...d6 {This is the most common move here. It happens in almost 50% of the games.} 9.Qa4+ {But it is a huge blunder! Qa4+ wins a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...d6 {Black chooses to go for a more solid approach with 3...d6, supporting the e5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. This happens in around 13% of the games. Let's push here. c3 followed by d4 is always the plan.} 4.d4 4...exd4 {Black trades, which is the most common continuation, happening in more than half of the games. Recapturing with the pawn to build the center and free up the c3-square.} 5.cxd4 5...Bg4 {By pinning our knight on f3, Black pressures the d4-pawn. But we simply develop the knight!} 6.Nc3 6...Bxf3 {Black trades. We've damaged our kingside structure, but the strong center proves to be a better asset in this position. Black is underdeveloped.} 7.gxf3 7...Nf6 {Black develops. Supporting the center and developing a piece.} 8.Be3 8...Be7 {Black prepares to castle... ...this shows why recapturing on f3 with the g-pawn is an advantage here. Our rook attacks Black's g7-pawn. Castling would be lethal.} 9.Rg1 9...g6 {Black covers it.} 10.Qb3 {White is doing great here. The b7-pawn is under pressure, and Black's king isn't sure which side to castle on. In the meantime, we can castle queenside and push f4-f5 with a strong grip over the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: Black's Other 3rd Moves / Study 1: 3...Bc5 and 3...d6 / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/a8287d43-e870-414f-bfb9-a0ddaa6d77e7/studies/a124ac23-ace7-48a6-9083-ba51c71ca46a/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black replies symmetrically. We develop.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. With c3 we go for The Ponziani, preparing to expand quickly with d4 and gain control over the center.} 3.c3 3...d6 {Black chooses to go for a more solid approach with 3...d6, supporting the e5-pawn and opening up the light-squared bishop. This happens in around 13% of the games. Let's push here. c3 followed by d4 is always the plan.} 4.d4 4...Bg4 {Black immediately pins our knight, the second most popular move after ...exd4, happening in 30% of the games. In this line, it's important to play quick and aggressive moves and put as much pressure on Black's pieces as we can while developing. Let's gain space in the center.} 5.d5 5...Nce7 {Black retreats. Asking a question to the bishop on g4.} 6.h3 6...Bh5 {Black keeps the pin. We free up the c3-square and support the d5-pawn. We'll be able to get the knight to c3 on the next move.} 7.c4 7...Nf6 {Black develops and attacks the pawn. White develops as well.} 8.Nc3 8...c6 {Black starts to chip away at our central structure. We develop the bishop.} 9.Be3 9...cxd5 {Black trades. We recapture.} 10.cxd5 10...Qa5 {Black pins our knight on c3, which means that the e4-pawn is hanging. We develop with a check. This tempo move is crucial so that we can castle and unpin.} 11.Bb5+ 11...Kd8 {Black moves the king. After 11...Nd7, we could win material with the neat trick 12.Bxd7+ Kxd7 13.Nxe5+! We get our king into safety.} 12.O-O 12...Nxe4 {Black uses a tactic to win a pawn... ...we'll gladly trade.} 13.Nxe4 13...Qxb5 {Black recaptures the piece.} 14.Nxd6 {But we win the pawn back! We've got yet again a great position with many weaknesses in Black's camp including their king.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...dxe4 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/d490c58a-477d-497e-ba42-985bd37e261e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 has been played in more than 2 million games, but few players know the actual theory at sub-2000 levels. Black wants to challenge the center right away and hopes to activate the queen on d5 when we can't attack it with Nc3. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...dxe4 {Nearly 47% of games go like this. White takes on e4. This move is really bad and shows that most people don't know the theory in this line. We won't recapture on e4. We will attack the pinned piece and take on e5.} 5.Nxe5 5...Bd7 {5...Bd7 is the most popular: Black unpins and threatens the knight. However, we can take and then take on e4. We will be up a pawn.} 6.Nxd7 6...Qxd7 {Black recaptures. And we are up a pawn.} 7.Qxe4+ 7...Be7 {This line is an example of how play could continue. We push with d4 and gain more space. We've cleared the center so this is a must.} 8.d4 8...Nf6 {Black attacks our queen. But they do not have nearly enough compensation for the material. We are up a central pawn.} 9.Qf3 9...O-O-O {Castling queenside is also bad. Black is down material but they allow us to develop with tempo.} 10.Bd3 {Black is lost. We have a simple threat. If Black defends with something like ...g6, we can continue with O-O and Bf4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...dxe4 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/d490c58a-477d-497e-ba42-985bd37e261e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 has been played in more than 2 million games, but few players know the actual theory at sub-2000 levels. Black wants to challenge the center right away and hopes to activate the queen on d5 when we can't attack it with Nc3. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...dxe4 {Nearly 47% of games go like this. White takes on e4. This move is really bad and shows that most people don't know the theory in this line. We won't recapture on e4. We will attack the pinned piece and take on e5.} 5.Nxe5 5...Qd5 {The second most popular move worth looking at is 5...Qd5. It is played in about 15% of games. With this move, Black defends their knight and attacks ours. But we can simply take and double the pawns. If 6...Bd7 we can play 7.Nb4.} 6.Nxc6 6...bxc6 {Black has to recapture with the pawn. Can you see why? We can now develop and attack the queen.} 7.Bc4 7...Qd6 {Black goes to d6, which appears to be safe. We will castle kingside. This is the prelude to all the pressure we will place on them.} 8.O-O 8...Nf6 {Black develops their knight, but now we can attack. We challenge the e4-pawn.} 9.d3 9...exd3 {Black takes on d3.} 10.Bf4 {Before Re1+, we have 10.Bf4 to attack the queen. Black can't capture because of Bxf7+. Our advantage increased quickly and we are much better. This is what happens if Black doesn't know how to handle 3...d5. We also have tactical ideas of Bxd3 where Black cannot take because of Qxc6+.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 1: Black Plays 4...dxe4 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/d490c58a-477d-497e-ba42-985bd37e261e/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 has been played in more than 2 million games, but few players know the actual theory at sub-2000 levels. Black wants to challenge the center right away and hopes to activate the queen on d5 when we can't attack it with Nc3. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...dxe4 {Nearly 47% of games go like this. White takes on e4. This move is really bad and shows that most people don't know the theory in this line. We won't recapture on e4. We will attack the pinned piece and take on e5.} 5.Nxe5 5...Bd7 {5...Bd7 is the most popular: Black unpins and threatens the knight. However, we can take and then take on e4. We will be up a pawn.} 6.Nxd7 6...Qxd7 {Black recaptures. And we are up a pawn.} 7.Qxe4+ 7...Be7 {This line is an example of how play could continue. We push with d4 and gain more space. We've cleared the center so this is a must.} 8.d4 8...Nf6 {Black attacks our queen. But they do not have nearly enough compensation for the material. We are up a central pawn.} 9.Qf3 9...O-O {Castling kingside is bad.} 10.Be2 {We can block the e-file and get ready to castle. With our pawn advantage there's not much to strategize. Play the position and win, using the e-file and the e5-square to get a piece in the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...f6 {4...f6, played in around 12% of games, is cutting edge theory. Black supports the center with the pawn and isn't afraid of weakening their king. We will play 5.d3 to support e4.} 5.d3 5...Be6 {The bishop helps support d5 and it clears the way for castling queenside, potentially. Since our pawn is on d3, we can only develop to e2.} 6.Be2 6...Qd7 {Queen to d7 paves the way for queenside castling. These positions are generally equal because Black's center is solid. We castle.} 7.O-O 7...O-O-O {Black castles queenside, which falls right into our strategy.} 8.b4 {And one of the benefits of c3 is to play b4, too. We will attack the king that is now stuck on the queenside behind all its clumsy pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...Bd7 {4...Bd7 breaks the pin. It has been played in around 20% of games. But we can take on d5 since the discovered attack isn't a problem.} 5.exd5 5...Nd4 {5...Nd4 is played almost 72% of times. It's a discovered attack on the queen but we also have to take care of the c2-square. The d1-square is the only square for us to defend everything.} 6.Qd1 6...Nxf3+ {The knight takes on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxf3 7...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 with pressure on d5. ...Bg4 also becomes possible. That's why we must defend d5.} 8.Bc4 8...Bg4 {Black attacks our queen. Luckily, our d5-pawn isn't completely defenseless. We can move away and attack the e5-pawn.} 9.Qe3 9...Bd6 {When Black defends it, but they also block their queen's attack on d5. This is still better than giving us the e5-pawn.} 10.d3 {Once again, 10.d3 is the best way to develop here because our queen is rather restricted on e3. We have to defend e4 and make sure we have dominant control over that square. We want to get a knight to e4, castle and play h3. We have a good position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...Bd7 {4...Bd7 breaks the pin. It has been played in around 20% of games. But we can take on d5 since the discovered attack isn't a problem.} 5.exd5 5...Nd4 {5...Nd4 is played almost 72% of times. It's a discovered attack on the queen but we also have to take care of the c2-square. The d1-square is the only square for us to defend everything.} 6.Qd1 6...Nxf3+ {The knight takes on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxf3 7...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 with pressure on d5. ...Bg4 also becomes possible. That's why we must defend d5.} 8.Bc4 8...Bd6 {With 8...Bd6, Black supports the e5-pawn and prepares castling. We push 9.d3 to control the e4-square. It will also be the funnel through which our pieces are developed.} 9.d3 9...O-O {Black castles.} 10.Bg5 {We don't mind giving up the bishop here because our control of the key e4-square will increase. We want to castle, play Nd2 and potentially even the move a4 if it's useful for queenside space and our bishop's comfort on c4.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...Bd7 {4...Bd7 breaks the pin. It has been played in around 20% of games. But we can take on d5 since the discovered attack isn't a problem.} 5.exd5 5...Nd4 {5...Nd4 is played almost 72% of times. It's a discovered attack on the queen but we also have to take care of the c2-square. The d1-square is the only square for us to defend everything.} 6.Qd1 6...Nxf3+ {The knight takes on f3. We recapture with the queen.} 7.Qxf3 7...Nf6 {Black develops their knight to f6 with pressure on d5. ...Bg4 also becomes possible. That's why we must defend d5.} 8.Bc4 8...e4 {Our queen can be attacked by the pawn. On e2 we can exert pressure on the e4-pawn.} 9.Qe2 9...Bd6 {Black brings the bishop out. We can push and gain some space because the e-pawn is pinned. Black is ahead in development for now, but our space advantage in the center restricts their potential.} 10.d4 10...O-O {Black castles. We'll play Bg5. Only good things can result from this pin. It's pressure that gives Black something to constantly worry about.} 11.Bg5 11...h6 {If ...h6, we don't take on f6. We keep the pin and try to get Black to push ...g5.} 12.Bh4 12...Re8 {The rook goes to e8 to defend e4 and potentially support the e3-push.} 13.O-O {And we castle. We want Nd2 and Rae1 because our rook on f1 may become useful if Black chooses to play ...e3 to open up the files.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...Bd7 {4...Bd7 breaks the pin. It has been played in around 20% of games. But we can take on d5 since the discovered attack isn't a problem.} 5.exd5 5...Nce7 {This is played less often.} 6.Qb3 {We can fall back and pressure b7. We are up a pawn and have a substantial advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...f6 {4...f6, played in around 12% of games, is cutting edge theory. Black supports the center with the pawn and isn't afraid of weakening their king. We will play 5.d3 to support e4.} 5.d3 5...dxe4 {5...dxe4 is natural. Black wants to trade. We recapture. Our bishop on f1 now has more squares to move to.} 6.dxe4 6...Bd7 {This unpins the knight and prepares a discovered attack.} 7.Qc2 {We of course get out of the way. We have an equal position on our hands but we will continue development with Bc4, O-O, Be3 and Nd2.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...f6 {4...f6, played in around 12% of games, is cutting edge theory. Black supports the center with the pawn and isn't afraid of weakening their king. We will play 5.d3 to support e4.} 5.d3 5...Be6 {The bishop helps support d5 and it clears the way for castling queenside, potentially. Since our pawn is on d3, we can only develop to e2.} 6.Be2 6...Bd6 {This is one of the potential setups that you could see. Both bishops out and supporting the central pawns. Our knight also supports e4. We can make use of e4 after all the central pawns are traded.} 7.Nbd2 7...Ne7 {Black develops the other knight to play O-O. Now breaking in the center is thematic. If you see that Black will castle kingside, d4 is a good plan.} 8.d4 8...exd4 {This trade also leads to another stable position. We recapture with the knight, centralizing it.} 9.Nxd4 9...Bd7 {The bishop goes and unpins the knight. But there isn't a scary discovered attack, so we can just castle.} 10.O-O 10...Nxd4 {Black can take on d4, attacking our queen.} 11.Qxd4 {But we can simply recapture. This is an example of a line Black has to know in order to have a decent position in The Ponziani. All of our pieces are ready to handle any trades in the center and attacks.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Masters Main Line (3...d5) / Study 2: Black Plays 4...Bd7 or 4...f6 / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/fcd36ab0-9705-4e69-9875-831929ac511b/chapters/51fd6140-dac4-43bb-98eb-1a5582d70157/studies/66674fad-6911-4ab8-8fc4-81a0710c6e13/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black pushes their e-pawn, too. We attack the e5-pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends. And now we go for The Ponziani.} 3.c3 3...d5 {3...d5 challenges our e4-pawn. We will respond with a pin on the c6-knight. Here, we also defend e4.} 4.Qa4 4...f6 {4...f6, played in around 12% of games, is cutting edge theory. Black supports the center with the pawn and isn't afraid of weakening their king. We will play 5.d3 to support e4.} 5.d3 5...Be6 {The bishop helps support d5 and it clears the way for castling queenside, potentially. Since our pawn is on d3, we can only develop to e2.} 6.Be2 6...Bd6 {This is one of the potential setups that you could see. Both bishops out and supporting the central pawns. Our knight also supports e4. We can make use of e4 after all the central pawns are traded.} 7.Nbd2 7...Ne7 {Black develops the other knight to play O-O. Now breaking in the center is thematic. If you see that Black will castle kingside, d4 is a good plan.} 8.d4 8...dxe4 {8...dxe4 is possible, but it's not best. We will get our knight to e4 quicker. We take, with an attack on the bishop.} 9.Nxe4 9...O-O {Black castles. We take in the center. Our knight is strong on e4, and so we don't want to take on d6 right away. We can do that later.} 10.dxe5 10...Nxe5 {Black takes on e5 with their knight.} 11.O-O {And we castle. We also don't mind ...Nxf3+, as we'll just recapture with the bishop. This is a stable position in which we'll enjoy some play, thanks to the incoming Rd1.} *